1	//! `nest` and `unnest` — grouping (spec §6, table "Clips").
2	//!
3	//! The two halves of one idea: a selection of clips becomes ONE clip on the
4	//! timeline showing a [`Nest`], and a nest clip becomes the clips again.
5	//! Their own module because grouping is a different concern from placement
6	//! — no other op changes which CONTAINER a clip lives in — and because the
7	//! pair only makes sense read together.
8	//!
9	//! ### They are inverses, up to ids
10	//!
11	//! `nest` then `unnest` gives back the clips it took: same sources, same
12	//! relative times, same props, same actions, same stacking. The ids differ,
13	//! exactly as they do across `split` then a re-join, because `unnest` mints
14	//! fresh ones — it has to, since a second clip may still show the same nest
15	//! and the clips inside it cannot be in two places at once.
16	//!
17	//! Two asymmetries are deliberate, and both come from the schema:
18	//!
19	//! - **A trimmed nest clip gives everything back.** `Source::Nest` has no in
20	//!   point (spec §5.2), so `unnest` is the inverse of `nest`, not of the
21	//!   trim that followed it: content the nest clip's edges were hiding
22	//!   reappears rather than being destroyed. Nothing is lost, and the user
23	//!   can trim again.
24	//! - **A clip the overlap rule refuses on the way out goes on a new track in
25	//!   front**, the fallback `paste` and Add Text already use (spec §6): a
26	//!   round trip lands on the tracks it came from because the nest clip's own
27	//!   span is free once it is removed, and only a nest that has since been
28	//!   moved next to other material needs the fallback. Refusing the whole op
29	//!   would leave the user with a group they cannot open.
30	//!
31	//! ### What `nest` decides
32	//!
33	//! The nest clip lands on the FRONT-most track the selection occupied that
34	//! is not a matte track (the front-most when all are, spec §11 MT-5), which
35	//! is where the selection's front-most DRAWN layer already was, and runs from
36	//! the earliest start to the latest end. The nest's own canvas is frozen at
37	//! the project's current size, so a later `set_canvas` cannot silently re-lay
38	//! out what is inside it ([`Nest`]). Its tracks are fresh — new ids, the
39	//! source tracks' names and their `hidden`/`muted`/`locked`/`solo` flags —
40	//! because the timeline's tracks stay on the timeline, holding whatever was
41	//! not selected. They copy each source track's `volume` and `pan` too, except
42	//! the copy of the nest clip's own destination track, which is unity and
43	//! centre: that track still applies over the nest clip, so a selection off one
44	//! track sounds the same nested, and the other source tracks' clips now also
45	//! get the destination's volume and pan on top of their own, as they get its
46	//! mute (spec §11 AU-102). `unnest` keeps neither, as it keeps no mute. A nested track keeps its source's `matte` only when the
47	//! nested track directly behind it is the copy of that matte's target (the
48	//! CARRY rule): a matte pair nested together goes on cutting inside the nest,
49	//! and a matte taken without its target becomes an ordinary track there.
50	//!
51	//! `unnest` lines the nest's front-most NON-matte track up with the nest
52	//! clip's track, so carried matte tracks land on the tracks in front of it
53	//! and nest-then-unnest gives the picture back; it makes no track a matte.
54	
55	use std::collections::HashMap;
56	
57	use crate::edit_adjustment::{adjustment_cannot_nest, is_adjustment};
58	use crate::edit_clip::clip_end;
59	use crate::edit_commit::Landing;
60	use crate::edit_link::relink_copies;
